<question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>U1 Pretest 01 U1L1-F01 Reconstruction</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Which statement best defines <strong>Reconstruction</strong> after the Civil War?]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Reconstruction addressed reunion and the political, legal, social, and economic meaning of freedom after slavery.]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><hidden>0</hidden><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>ABCD</answernumbering><showstandardinstruction>0</showstandardinstruction>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The process of restoring the Union and defining and protecting freedom after slavery]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The military campaign to capture Confederate capitals before secession]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[This describes warfare, not Reconstruction.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The permanent division of the United States into Union and Confederate nations]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Reconstruction sought reunion, not permanent division.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The movement to expand slavery into every western territory]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Reconstruction followed slavery's destruction.]]></text></feedback></answer>
  </question>
<question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>U1 Pretest 02 U1L1-S01 Emancipation Proclamation Scope</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Source:</strong> Emancipation Proclamation, January 1, 1863</p><blockquote>“all persons held as slaves within any State or designated part of a State, the people whereof shall then be in rebellion ... shall be then, thenceforward, and forever free”</blockquote><p>Which conclusion is best supported by the quoted language?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The wording targeted areas in rebellion, revealing the proclamation's wartime scope.]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><hidden>0</hidden><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>ABCD</answernumbering><showstandardinstruction>0</showstandardinstruction>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The proclamation used wartime federal authority against slavery in designated rebellious areas.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The proclamation immediately abolished slavery in every state without exception.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The quoted scope is limited to designated areas in rebellion.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The proclamation restored Confederate state governments.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Restoration is not established by this passage.]]></text></feedback></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The proclamation defined national citizenship and voting rights.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The passage does not define citizenship or suffrage.]]></text></feedback></answer>
  </question>
